hi i'm derek downey a developer advocate  for google cloud  i am excited to share some great news if  you are using cloud spanner and have  downstream systems that need to be aware  of data changes in near real time  getting data changes from one system  into another especially in real time can  be challenging  to address this cloud spanner just  released change streams to make it easy  to set up pipelines to process changes  made to your spanner data  in this video i will explain what chain  streams are and what is required to set  them up  then i will show you how easy it is to  set up a change stream and a pipeline to  process changes made to your spanner  data into a downstream bigquery  analytics environment  finally i'll go over some typical use  cases for change streams and how spanner  makes it easy to enable those use cases  [Music]  so what are change streams  if you have ever heard of change data  capture or cdc that's a good starting  point for understanding change streams  in cloud spanner  change streams keep track of changes  made to data through dml or spanner  mutations that can then be processed by  pipelines to publish in a downstream  system  there are many reasons you might want to  get data changes from one system into  another you might have a data warehouse  and you need real-time information to  improve business decisions  you might want to trigger downstream  application workflows for instance  restocking an item if the inventory  level drops beneath a threshold  you might have a need to keep an audit  trail of changes in a separate system  for compliance purposes  or perhaps you want to keep track of  changes for disaster recovery  whatever the reason the process to set  up a change stream in spanner is  straightforward  first create the change stream in your  database  a change stream is another type of  object in spanner like a table or  secondary index  so you can add modify or delete change  stream objects using ddl  you can create a single change stream  for all tables in a database or you can  create a change stream for specific  tables or even specific columns within  those tables  second you need a destination to receive  the changes  this can be bigquery google cloud  storage google pub sub or any other  system to receive the stream of changes  and finally you set up a pipeline to  process change events from the change  stream and spanner and publish to your  downstream system you can easily build  data flow pipelines to process the  change stream events leveraging spanner  io from the apache beam repository  or you can connect directly to the  spanner api and write your own  processing logic  this might be best to explain with an  example  i currently have an inventory service  for an e-commerce store that is backed  by spanner  i also have bigquery set up to track  inventory behavior over time having this  analytics environment has enabled me to  make good decisions on what items to  keep in stock  currently to get data from spanner into  bigquery i had to set up and maintain  additional pipelines to extract the data  in batches before loading into bigquery  these batches cause some delays that  have caused headaches for my analysts  with change streams i can move to almost  real time streaming of data changes into  bigquery and simplify the logic for  processing  to set up my change stream i go into the  cloud console page for my spanner  instance  then i add my change stream via the ddl  page  the syntax is fairly straightforward  you need a change stream name  change stream names are unique to the  spanner database  next you specify the entities you want  to track in this change stream  you can track the entire database a set  of tables or specific columns for tables  last you specify the retention period  for change stream data  the records that are tracked are stored  for this retention period before  automatically cleaning up  by default the retention period is 24  hours but can range up to 7 days  and you'll pay only for the additional  storage required to keep the change  events longer  now i also have my bigquery environment  set up that is used for inventory  analytics  all i have to do is set up the dataflow  pipeline for bigquery  so i go into dataflow in the cloud  console then create a new pipeline i  provide the pipeline and name  the region i select is us central 1 to  be near my spanner instance  and then i select the cloud spanner to  bigquery template  this template leverages the apache beam  spanner i o connector  here i need to provide some more  information  first is the spanner instance name and  database name  this is the location of the chain stream  object i created earlier  next i need to provide a metadata  instance and database name  this is needed for the spanner io  connector to keep track of the changes  it has processed  the metadata instance and database can  be the same as where the change stream  is located but i'd recommend at least  storing the metadata in its own database  once we've provided the metadata  location then we provide the change  stream name  and finally i specify a bigquery data  set to store the data changes  there are some optional parameters but  this is enough to get started processing  the change streams from when the  pipeline is created  that's it after a few minutes for the  pipeline to get set up i will start  receiving data changes into bigquery  as part of releasing change streams the  spanner team has also released spanner  io to the apache beam repository  spanner io can be used to process data  change events from spanner change  streams  there are also three main data flow  pipeline templates available that  leverage spanner io to quickly get  started processing  spanner change streams  the first is the bigquery template that  i just walked through  this allows you to set up bigquery to  receive changes from spanner in near  real time  there is also a google cloud storage  template  one example of sending data changes to  cloud storage would be to keep an audit  archive of detailed change events on  important data for regulatory purposes  and finally there is a template to  publish data changes to pub sub  this is useful to trigger workflows in  external applications that are listening  on a downstream pub sub related to those  changes  if you need to publish data changes to  another destination or if you need  additional processing of the data  changes then you can create a custom  pipeline that leverages the apache beam  spanner i o connector  it is also possible to connect directly  to the change stream api  to create a customized data pipeline  without using the spanner i o connector  it's possible to have multiple change  streams in the same database and even  multiple change streams tracking the  same tables or columns  here are some things to keep in mind  with multiple change streams  you can have up to 10 change streams per  database and up to three chain streams  can track the same non-key column  you should maintain no more than five  concurrent readers for a change stream  partition  or performance made to grade  change streams is a great new feature in  spanner that enables real-time data  pipelines based on data changes  i have shown how to set up a basic  pipeline to get spanner data changes  into bigquery using the available  dataflow template there are many ways  change streams can be leveraged from  triggering downstream application  workflows using pub sub storing data  within cloud storage to enable data  recovery or for setting up an audit  trail for compliance purposes  let us know in the comments how you're  using change streams and if there are  other templates you'd like us to provide  in the future  [Music]  you
 

